{
  "description": "JS/Python PII regex parity fixture (orchestrator, 2026-09-24). Every case is run through extension/lib/pii-regex.js findRegexSpans() (tests/unit/test_pii_parity.mjs) AND server/pii_regex_v2.py find_regex_spans() (tests/unit/test_pii_parity.py); expected is derived from the JS implementation (the more extensively hand-verified side across V1a's prior work, including an empirical probe that corrected two wrong-by-hand predictions of this fixture's own author -- see the two ids below whose text still carries an outdated hypothesis in their id/description: they are accurate). Covers every C1 regex label (EMAIL, PHONE, AADHAAR, PAN, CARD, UPI, IFSC, GSTIN, PASSPORT) with positives and near-miss negatives, all four documented ordering hazards (card-before-phone/aadhaar, GSTIN-before-PAN, UPI-before-PHONE, IFSC-before-PASSPORT), every phone prefix form (+91, 0091, 91-, leading-0 trunk, generic international bare and grouped), UPI with both a common (formerly-allowlisted) and a deliberately arbitrary/uncommon handle (proving server/pii_regex_v2.py's PSP-handle allowlist removal), emails/UPI-lookalikes in both directions, and token-wrapped strings that must yield zero spans under the server's masking mode (find_regex_spans masks tokens first; the client-side findRegexSpans() never needs to, since it only ever runs on raw pre-tokenization text in production -- these specific fixtures happen to yield zero spans in BOTH regardless, because no token-shaped substring accidentally matches any PII pattern). LANDLINE FOLLOW-UP (same day): added a shared JS/Python false negative for Indian landline numbers (STD 2-4 digits + subscriber 6-8 digits, always exactly 10 significant digits) in hyphen/paren/space-grouped and +91/91-/bare-trunk-0 forms, near-miss negatives (ISO dates, 6-digit pincodes, time ranges), and two non-splitting cases proving landline cannot steal a substring from an already-claimed mobile number, a genuinely Luhn-valid card, or an AADHAAR grouped match.",
